Reconstructing individual sovereignty in the cyber‑psychology era demands a paradigm shift from  _caveat emptor_  (let the buyer be cautious) to  _caveat venditor_  (let the seller be obligated to protect), as emphasized by Zuboff (2019). The State, through administrative and civil instruments, must mandate radical transparency for every AI system that interacts affectively with humans. Systems must not be permitted to masquerade as entities possessing feelings, allowing users to become lost in an illusion of artificial consciousness without clear, periodic structural reminders of the system’s mechanical nature (Floridi, 2013). Moreover, data‑protection safeguards under frameworks such as the Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act (Law No. 27/2022) must be expanded to cover “psychological and emotional profiling”—preventing corporations from collecting, mapping, and commercialising individuals’ affective vulnerability maps for the sake of commercial retention optimization (Farahany, 2023).

Ultimately, the final bulwark of human sovereignty does not rest on digital code walls but on the restoration of critical autonomy through cognitive‑literacy education and legal protection that unequivocally upholds human dignity (Jonas, 1979). Law must not allow the human soul to become a commodity in the free market of fabricated intimacy algorithms (Weizenbaum, 1976). When technology penetrates the interiority of the mind and blurs the line between the real and the virtual, law must stand as an ethical fortress reaffirming that humans— with all their fragility, wounds, and limitations—are subjects entitled to an authentic reality, free from the invisible, systemic manipulations that threaten their autonomy (Buber, 1923; Levinas, 1961).

