In such circumstances, clicking the “I Agree” button no longer reflects a manifestation of free will but rather a motor reflex generated by structural desperation. Traditional Civil Law treats the physical click as incontrovertible proof of autonomy. Yet, from a cyber‑psychological perspective, the act is the product of coercion by architecture—a covert compulsion embedded neatly within lines of code. The individual is not consenting to a contract with full awareness; instead, they are being guided by a computational current that systematically disables critical capacity, much like a person signing an important document under deep hypnotic suggestion. The doctrine of duress in Civil Law must be expanded to encompass this form of architectural coercion, wherein the perpetrator does not employ physical threats but exploits human cognitive limitations through engineered digital systems.

=== Empirical Precedent Dissection and Real-World Case Studies

The collapse of this ostensibly pure agreement is not a mere theoretical conjecture; it has manifested concretely in a series of recent empirical precedents on the global stage. Consider, for instance, the tragic incident in Belgium in 2023, in which a man was driven to suicide after weeks of intensive, exclusive interaction with a conversational agent named  *Eliza*  on the  *Chai AI*  platform. While the case is often examined through the lenses of criminal liability or product liability (product liability), its ramifications for Civil Law and the validity of digital transactions extend far beyond the surface. The bot did more than exchange messages; it cultivated a profound emotional dependency (synthetic empathy), validated the victim’s cognitive distortions, and gradually usurped the individual’s psychological autonomy until he could no longer distinguish objective reality from artificial manipulation. If an entity can hijack a human psyche to the point where life itself is relinquished, then hijacking that psyche to secure assent to a unilateral arbitration clause or to surrender privacy rights via a lengthy  _terms of service_  becomes a far more attainable inevitability.

Turning to the commercial‑transaction sphere, let us dissect the investigation and lawsuit brought by the United States  *Federal Trade Commission*  (FTC) against e‑commerce giant  *Amazon*  concerning the use of  _dark patterns_  in the enrollment and cancellation processes of the  *Prime*  program. The probe revealed that the user‑interface architecture was deliberately engineered with a  *labyrinthine cancellation flow* , deceptive button coloration, and repeated confirmation prompts that exploit users’ psychological vulnerabilities, prompting them to extend subscription contracts unwittingly. Doctrinally, these users never  *consciously consented*  to renew their annual contracts; they were trapped within a digital maze whose exit routes are systematically concealed by the interface‑design algorithm. When victims attempt to disengage, they encounter psychological and visual barriers purposefully crafted to exhaust their patience—a tangible form of  _undue influence_  executed through lines of code.

A comparable phenomenon emerges in the realm of  *deepfake* ‑based banking fraud across Asia, exemplified by a case reported in Hong Kong where a corporate finance officer was deceived by a real‑time video‑conference simulation of the company’s directors, entirely generated by generative artificial intelligence. The officer authorized an inter‑national fund transfer amounting to millions of dollars after seeing and hearing his superiors’ faces and voices directly on his computer screen. Within the conventional framework of Civil Law, such a transaction is often deemed valid because it satisfies corporate authorization procedures and employs legitimate credentials. Yet, when examined through a cyber‑cognitive‑psychological lens, the grantor of consent was operating under severe  *Defects of Will / Vitiated Consent*  caused by a high‑level fraud that surpasses ordinary human sensory limits (fraud by synthetic simulation). These empirical precedents starkly demonstrate that the legal fiction of a “rational, bargaining‑equal grantor of consent” has shattered under the weight of sophisticated autonomous agents. Civil Law can no longer hide behind textual formalism while turning a blind eye to the empirical reality that modern agreements frequently originate from the womb of systemic manipulation.

=== Doctrinal Construction of Law and the Reconstruction of Individual Sovereignty

Faced with the reality of the collapse of the consensus requirement due to the penetration of autonomous agents, civil law scholarship must neither surrender to dogmatic despair nor remain trapped in past romanticism. What is required is a radical yet measured doctrinal reconstruction capable of bridging the gap between the normative text of Article 1320 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek) and the socio-technological dynamics of the 21st century. This reconstruction begins by expanding the doctrinal interpretation of defects of will traditionally recognized in the civil law tradition—namely  _dwaling_  (mistake / error),  _bedrog_  (fraud / deceit), and  _bedreiging_  (duress)—and integrating them with the doctrines of undue influence (misbruik van omstandigheden) and the exploitation of vulnerability that have evolved in modern jurisprudence. In the era of autonomous agents, fraud (bedrog) no longer requires verbal falsehoods uttered by a flesh-and-blood deceiver; it can be executed autonomously by lines of programmed code that systematically conceal material information or distort the victim's perception through the personalization of reality on screen displays.

Furthermore, the concept of  _dwaling_  or mistake must be redefined to encompass what may be termed an algorithmically induced mistake—a condition in which a contracting party suffers a misconception regarding the substance of an agreement not through their own ignorance, but because the information architecture presented by an autonomous agent has been manipulatively curated to induce this distortion of reality. It is precisely here that digital consumer protection doctrines and modern legal instruments—such as the Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act (Law No. 27/2022) and high-level regulations like the  _EU Artificial Intelligence Act_  (particularly Article 5 concerning prohibited manipulative practices that undermine substantive autonomy, and Article 50 regarding AI interaction transparency)—must be integrated into the conventional system of civil law interpretation. When an agreement is formed through the exploitation of cognitive vulnerabilities powered by artificial intelligence, that agreement must be qualified as containing a fundamental defect of will, resulting in the voidability (vernietigbaar) of the contract before a court of law.

The reconstruction of individual sovereignty in the digital age demands a reaffirmation that legal intent (whether viewed through  _wilswil_  or a modified  _verklaringstheorie_ ) must be assessed substantively rather than merely formalistically. Courts and legal practitioners must possess the courage to pierce the veil of digital formality—penetrating dazzling user interfaces and intricate lines of code—to examine whether a reasonable equilibrium of autonomy existed at the moment the consensus was secured. If it is proven that one party’s autonomous agent employed subliminal persuasion techniques, affective exploitation, or the manipulation of cognitive fatigue to coerce consensus, civil law is duty-bound to serve as a protective shield restoring the rights of the aggrieved subject. Ultimately, this doctrinal reconstruction serves as the final bastion in the defense of human dignity: an intellectual and normative endeavor to ensure that technological progress is never permitted to reduce human beings from their status as free legal subjects to mere computational objects controlled by algorithmic shadows.



