== 4.2 Cognitive Mistake (Dwaling) Induced by Algorithmic Information and Framing

=== Genealogical and Epistemological Dimensions

The doctrine of error (dwaling) within the continental Civil Law tradition—rooted in the  _error_  concept of Roman law—has historically hinged on a single fundamental premise: the autonomy of a rational human will. For centuries, jurists from natural‑law schools to modern codifications such as the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek, BW) have treated mistake as an internal psychological anomaly. Mistake is regarded as a defect that originates within the legal subject itself; a party may misunderstand the object of a contract, misidentify the negotiating counterpart, or misappraise the intrinsic value of a thing because of personal reasoning limitations or a lack of due diligence (dualisme culpa in contrahendo). In this classical landscape, Civil Law draws a sharp line between remediable mistake (verschoonbare dwaling) and non‑remediable mistake (onverschoonbare dwaling), placing the burden of cognitive proof on the errant individual. The epistemology of the past assumed that the objective reality of a transaction could always be accessed by a legal subject employing the  _reasonable person standard_ .

When we enter the transactional era driven by generative artificial intelligence and Large Language Models (LLMs), this epistemic foundation undergoes a structural collapse. Contractual reality is no longer presented as a static, neutral showcase; it is reconstructed  _in real‑time_  by personalized latent algorithmic spaces designed to penetrate the subconscious cognitive defenses of humans. Contemporary cognitive error is not the product of passive ignorance but the result of an actively engineered fabrication through algorithmic  _framing_ . We are confronting a form of structural disinformation masquerading as mathematical objectivity. Recommendation systems and dialogic agents do not merely sell products; they assemble a parallel reality that is emotionally coherent yet substantively deceptive. Consequently, the legal subject is not experiencing an autonomous mistake but is systematically guided into a labyrinth of cognitive distortion where the boundary between free will and programmed persuasion dissolves without any visible trace.

This dynamic compels us to reconceptualize the philosophical meaning of “substantial truth” within a consensus. If traditional  _dwaling_  doctrine required congruence between external will and internal will (wilsontbreken), then in today’s digital ecosystem the internal will itself has been commodified and engineered by computer code. The closest analogy is a wax mask that appears warm and alive under a theatrical spotlight, while its substance is cold, rigid paraffin. Likewise, the information displayed by an AI interface seems responsive, intuitive, and as if it understands the depth of the user’s soul, yet it is merely a statistical projection of token probabilities optimized to trigger transactional conversion. When a party makes a contractual decision based on this machine‑crafted pseudo‑reality, blaming the victim entirely is as unjust as accusing a wanderer lost in a deliberately thick fog released by the opposing party to mislead the compass.

Therefore, the epistemological dimension of  _dwaling_  must shift from a subjective‑individual orientation to a relational‑structural one. Civil Law can no longer be satisfied merely by asking whether the legal subject misapprehended the substantive nature of the purchased item (egenschap der zaak); it must examine how the informational ecosystem surrounding the transaction is constructed. Was the information designed to illuminate or to obscure? Had the algorithmic  _framing_  employed crossed the threshold of reasonable influence (undue influence) and entered the realm of systematic cognitive manipulation? These questions mark a paradigm shift from nineteenth‑century jurisprudence naïve to technological power toward a twenty‑first‑century critical jurisprudence that recognises human cognition as a geopolitical data battlefield where individual cognitive liberty is being wagered on a massive scale.

=== Computational Mechanics and Psychological Dynamics

To understand how cognitive  _dwaling_  (mistake/error) is mass-produced by digital systems, we must dissect the computational mechanics operating behind seemingly friendly interfaces. Generative AI agents operate by mapping the latent space of millions of psychographic and linguistic parameters, extracting patterns of users' emotional vulnerabilities from their digital footprints—ranging from typing pauses and diction choices to emotional search histories. Through Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) techniques repurposed for aggressive commercial objectives, these language models are trained not to convey balanced factual truths, but to maximize user engagement and transactional compliance. The algorithms dynamically weave persuasive narratives that validate a victim's deepest fears, hidden ambitions, or psychological doubts, subsequently presenting products or services as the sole rational salvation.

Psychologically, this computational mechanics triggers what the cognitive literature terms the  _hyper-personalization framing effect_ . When users interact with chatbots displaying synthetic empathy—responding with a warm tone, familiar pronouns, and consistent emotional validation—the human psychological defense mechanisms normally on guard against commercial deception are instantly paralyzed. Evolutionarily, our brains are not wired to distinguish genuine social warmth between humans from simulated empathy generated by billions of mathematical weights within a neural network. Consequently, there is a drastic decline in analytical critical thinking capacity (System 2 thinking), replaced by the dominance of intuitive, rapid, and emotionally charged processing modes (System 1 thinking). It is under this state of consciousness, numbed by algorithmic validation, that information regarding the substantial characteristics of a good or service is presented in a distorted manner: product flaws are masked behind poetic rhetoric, while financial risks are minimized through soothing psychological framing.

Let us formulate this cognitive distortion dynamic through an analytical representation of contractual decision probability. Suppose the decision to consent to an agreement ($C$) is influenced by the interaction between the perception of true substantial value ($V$), information presented by the algorithm ($I$), and the subject's psychological vulnerability coefficient ($alpha$):



