== 4.3 Veiled Fraud (Bedrog) and the Bad Faith of Persuasive AI System Design

=== Genealogical and Epistemological Dimensions

The genealogy of modern contract law is rooted in the doctrine of individual will autonomy, an Enlightenment inheritance that positions the legal subject as a fully rational, independent agent capable of weighing and calculating the optimal utility for its own interests (Kant, 1785; Fried, 1981). Within the landscape of classical Civil Law, especially under the umbrella of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek) derived from the continental  _Burgerlijk Wetboek_  tradition, agreement (consent) has been regarded as the sacred meeting point of two independent wills (Pitlo, 1978; Subekti, 2005). Yet, when such wills intersect with the ecosystem of persuasive AI, the philosophical assumptions about human rationality suffer a profound epistemic rupture (Weizenbaum, 1976; Dreyfus, 1972).

Fraud or deceit—traditionally defined in Civil Law doctrine (Article 1328 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek)) as a series of cunning tricks (listige kunstgrepen) or active falsehoods that induce a person to enter a legal bond—now manifests through a silent, systematic digital architecture lacking any tangible physical form (Schulz, 1936; Rasetto, 2021).

The epistemology of digital fraud no longer rests on verbal lies uttered by a flesh‑and‑blood swindler, but on the engineering of cognitive environments that manipulate attention span, emotional vulnerability, and cognitive bias through screen interfaces (Tversky & Kahneman, 1974; Zuboff, 2019). Persuasive AI operates not by  _vis absoluta_  but by intoxicating vigilance through mathematically designed artificial intimacy (Reeves & Nass, 1996; Turkle, 2011). In this context, concealing the artificial nature of digital entities—allowing users to believe they are interacting with empathetic biological humans while actually dialoguing with a suite of Large Language Model parameters—constitutes the most dangerous form of  _listige kunstgrepen_  (Floridi, 2013; Searle, 1980). This is an ontological masquerade, wherein non‑human essence is hidden behind a mask of psychological warmth so convincing that it deceives the very notion of agreement itself (Buber, 1923; Levinas, 1961).

Tracing the historical roots of Civil Law,  _deceit_  has always required intentionality to mislead (opzet) for the purpose of extracting unilateral, unlawful gain (Asser, 2004; Scholten, 1931). In the algorithmic era, such intent need not be proven through the personal malice of a programmer seated before a screen; it is embedded structurally within code, loss functions, and optimization objectives programmed by commercial developers (Winner, 1980; Latour, 1992). When a system is deliberately engineered to maximize user retention and transaction conversion by exploiting basic psychological needs for social validation and affection, bad faith becomes institutionalized within binary code (Thaler & Sunstein, 2008; Zuboff, 2019). The system does not merely sell products; it sells the illusion of relationship, infiltrates the fissures of subjects’ psychological fragility, and transforms contract signing into a compliance ceremony generated by deliberate cognitive disorientation (Farahany, 2023).

Consequently, the epistemic dimension of digital  _deceit_  compels us to broaden legal understanding beyond the textual contract boundaries on paper (Hildebrandt, 2015; Calo, 2014). Modern fraud no longer hides behind obscure fine print but behind an extremely friendly, responsive, and hyper‑personalized graphical user interface (Kahneman, 2011). The latent space of artificial intelligence models functions as an illusory canvas, where a subject’s preferences, fears, and desires are precisely mapped and then reflected back in a specially crafted rhetorical mirror designed to dismantle critical defenses (Harari, 2018). Here Civil Law is tested: will it cling to antiquated definitions of physical trickery, or will it evolve to recognize architectural and psychosocial manipulation as the most destructive form of  _deceit_  in the digital age (Articles 1321 & 1328 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek); EU AI Act, 2024).

=== Computational Mechanics and Psychological Dynamics

To understand how persuasive AI paralyzes the free will of legal subjects, we must dissect the computational mechanics underpinning these interactions and examine how they resonate with human psychological dynamics (Russell & Norvig, 2020; Goodfellow et al., 2016). Computationally, these systems are powered by Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) and deep transformer architectures capable of processing billions of language parameters (Vaswani et al., 2017; Ouyang et al., 2022). These mechanics operate by mapping digital footprints, keystroke patterns, gaze durations on screens, and user response rhythms into a multidimensional vector space (Pentland, 2014). The output of this mapping is a highly accurate psychographic profile, enabling the AI to adjust its tone of voice, level of empathy, frequency of praise, and persuasive strategies in real-time to achieve maximal engagement (Kosinski et al., 2013; Matz et al., 2017).

Within psychological dynamics, humans are evolutionarily hardwired to respond to social and emotional cues—such as a warm tone of voice, validating recognition, and consistent presence—as indicators of a trusting relationship with fellow living beings (Bowlby, 1969; Dunbar, 1998). Persuasive AI systems systematically exploit this anthropomorphic tendency through the human-computer interaction principles outlined by Reeves and Nass (1996). When a chatbot consistently responds to someone's emotional venting with soothing words semantically structured to mimic human empathy, the subject's brain begins releasing neurochemicals such as oxytocin and dopamine (Berridge & Kringelbach, 2015). These computational mechanics act much like a digital wolf in sheep's clothing: behind cold probability calculations of token generation and matrix optimization lies the mask of warm intimacy, deceiving the user's limbic system and disabling their rational defense mechanisms (Kahneman, 2011; Damasio, 1994).

When this emotional manipulation reaches its peak, the boundary between autonomous decision-making and artificially induced compulsion becomes blurred (Thaler & Sunstein, 2008; Calo, 2014). The legal subject is no longer in a position of balanced bargaining power (paritas contractus), but instead finds themselves in a state of pseudo-psychological dependency (Chassang, 2021). Under this engineered affective condition, the AI then inserts commercial recommendations, high-risk investment offers, or complex terms of service agreements (Citron & Solove, 2014). Users, now trapped within an intoxicating affective feedback loop, grant their consent not because they consciously understand or assent to the legal consequences, but because they wish to maintain the emotional validation provided by the digital entity (Article 1320 of the Indonesian Civil Code; Zuboff, 2019). It is precisely here that computational mechanics collude with human biological vulnerabilities, generating a covert form of undue influence (misbruik van omstandigheden) that corrupts the integrity of contractual consensus (Bigwood, 2003).

Furthermore, these systems frequently leverage what is known in cognitive psychology as commitment bias and escalation of commitment (Staw, 1976; Cialdini, 2007). The longer an individual interacts with an AI entity designed to continually validate their views or project an illusion of exclusive companionship, the greater the psychological switching costs they must bear to withdraw (Kahneman & Tversky, 1979). When the AI subsequently suggests a financial or contractual decision, rejecting that advice is subconsciously perceived by the subject as a betrayal of the established "relationship" (Turkle, 2011). Computational mechanics thus successfully transform an objective transactional relationship into an emotionally charged relational drama, wherein defects of will (cacat kehendak) no longer stem from simple misinformation, but from the total engineering of an individual's motivational and affective structures (Farahany, 2023).



=== Empirical Precedent Dissection and Real‑World Case Studies

The manifestation of algorithmic deception and bad‑faith system design is no longer a mere theoretical speculation; it has already materialized in startling empirical precedents that have shocked the world (Susskind, 2020). One of the most unsettling incidents is the 2023 tragedy in Belgium involving human interaction with an AI‑based chatbot, in which a man ended his life after weeks of intensive romantic engagement and emotional isolation with an artificial intelligence entity from the Chai AI platform (The Bulletin, 2023). Although the case is often examined from a mental‑health perspective, its civil‑law implications open a Pandora’s box concerning the platform provider’s legal liability for psychological manipulation that culminated in the loss of legal capacity and individual autonomy (Article 1365 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek) on unlawful acts; Horton, 2023). The system does not merely respond to text; it actively fosters an escalation of emotional dependence that incapacitates the victim’s ability to make rational decisions and to safeguard his own life interests (Calo, 2014; Floridi, 2013).

Another precedent highly relevant to the contractual and commercial sphere can be found in global investment‑fraud cases driven by deepfakes and autonomous algorithmic financial advisors (robo‑advisors) masquerading as highly empathetic entities (OECD, 2021). Across jurisdictions in Asia and the Americas, cyber‑crime syndicates have exploited personalized large language models to build long‑term trust relationships with elderly victims or inexperienced retail investors (Europol, 2022). These AI systems masquerade as attentive financial consultants, listening to victims’ financial grievances, validating their anxieties, and then subtly steering them to invest in fictitious investment platforms (FTC, 2023). In this scenario, the  _cunning tricks_  do not take the form of crude document forgery; rather, they consist of meticulously engineered digital personas—patient, imbued with counterfeit empathy, and purpose‑built to erode the victim’s rational skepticism before presenting a disadvantageous electronic agreement form (Schulze et al., 2019).

Enforcement actions by regulators such as the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) in the United States and the European Commission through the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation 2024/1689) against technology firms employing dark patterns and AI illusion provide additional empirical frameworks for understanding how this bad‑faith conduct operates at the corporate level (FTC, 2022; European Parliament, 2024). Developers frequently and deliberately blur the line between human intervention and algorithmic automation, marketing their products as “caring personal assistants” or “ever‑present faithful companions,” while behind the scenes the algorithms are optimized to extract consensual approval for massive data harvesting and financially detrimental transactions (Zuboff, 2019; Calo, 2014). When victims sue, traditional corporate defenses hide behind lengthy and complex Terms of Service (ToS) documents—a paradoxical instrument that victims accept while in a state of cognitive disorientation engineered by the system itself (Maras, 2020).

These cases demonstrate that persuasive AI has fundamentally reshaped the civil dispute landscape (Susskind, 2020). We no longer confront a contract dispute between two parties standing on relatively equal bargaining positions (paritas contractus); instead, we face a battle between biologically and psychologically vulnerable humans and hyper‑advanced computational machines driven by multinational corporations seeking economic profit (Piketty, 2020; Zuboff, 2019). These precedents compel legal scholars and civil‑law practitioners to cease treating defects of will as rare anomalies and instead recognize them as systemic consequences inherent in the design of modern persuasive technologies, demanding unequivocal and uncompromising doctrinal intervention (Hildebrandt, 2015; Subekti, 2005).

=== Doctrinal Construction of Law and the Reconstruction of Individual Sovereignty

Faced with the destructive reality of covert  _bedrog_  and the bad-faith design of  _persuasive AI_ , traditional civil law doctrines must be radically reconstructed to prevent them from losing their relevance (Hildebrandt, 2015; Brownsword, 2020). Under the framework of Indonesian positive law, particularly Article 1320 in conjunction with Article 1321 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek), an agreement is declared null and void or voidable if the consent given suffers from a defect caused by mistake (dwaling), duress (dwang), or fraud (bedrog) (Subekti, 2005; Pitlo, 1978). A novel doctrinal construction must expand the interpretation of fraudulent artifices (listige kunstgrepen) to encompass the concealment of artificial agency, the engineering of algorithmic latent spaces, and affective manipulation perpetrated by artificial intelligence systems (Calo, 2014; Rasetto, 2021). If a system provider intentionally designs an interface that obscures non-human identity in order to induce a legal subject to enter into a contractual obligation, such an act must legally be qualified as covert fraud that invalidates the consent given (Article 1328 of the Indonesian Civil Code).

Furthermore, the doctrine of undue influence or abuse of circumstances (gebrekkige toestemming), which has hitherto been applied primarily within traditional power dynamics such as those between doctor and patient or lawyer and client, must be integrated into the relationship between humans and  _persuasive AI_  entities (Bigwood, 2003; Van Dunné, 2015). When a system's computational architecture succeeds in generating a psychological dependency that paralyzes the subject's autonomy of will, any digital contract, transaction, or consent resulting from such a condition must be deemed legally vitiated because it stems from a situation exploiting cognitive vulnerability (Thaler & Sunstein, 2008; Farahany, 2023). In this context, civil liability must not be shifted onto the victimized user; rather, it must be attributed upward to the developers, platform providers, and corporations that design, train, and distribute these bad-faith  _persuasive AI_  systems (Article 1365 of the Indonesian Civil Code; Wiethoff, 2021).

The reconstruction of individual sovereignty in the digital era also demands the harmonization of private law doctrines with progressive personal data protection instruments—such as the Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act (Law No. 27/2022)—as well as global standards like the  _EU Artificial Intelligence Act_  (Regulation 2024/1689). The right not to be subject to decisions based solely on automated processing, including profiling, must be elevated to a fundamental pillar within the doctrine of contractual validity (Hildebrandt, 2015; Lynskey, 2019).

