In conclusion to this discourse, 21st-century civil law jurists face a historical summons to rescue the meaning of human autonomy from the clutches of surveillance capitalism and algorithmic manipulation (Zuboff, 2019; Susskind, 2020). The law must not remain passive, hiding behind obsolete dogmatic formalism while persuasive algorithms continually erode the foundations of free will that animate the private law system (Brownsword, 2020). By reforming the doctrines of  _bedrog_  and undue influence, and by upholding the principle of substantive justice above the illusion of digital consensus, we not only rescue individual victims from the snares of the digital wolf in sheep's clothing, but we also restore moral integrity and human dignity to the very heart of the modern civil law system (Jonas, 1979; Habermas, 1981).



