== 8.2 Sentiment Analysis, Affective Polarization, and the Detection of Algorithmic Gaslighting

In the realm of contemporary personal data protection, the global regulatory landscape is frequently ensnared in a narrow bureaucratic paradigm. In Indonesia, Law No. 27 of 2022 on Personal Data Protection (Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act)—architecturally aligned with the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) regime—has established a foundational milestone. It provides a legal instrument in the form of a data subject's right to object to decisions based solely on automated processing (automated individual decision-making), including profiling that produces legal consequences or similarly significant effects (Article 20, Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act). Nonetheless, this normative framework remains fixated on administrative, procedural, and material-ownership dimensions of data.

Our positive law has yet to touch the core of the cognitive vulnerabilities that emerge when waves of tokenized words—crafted by artificial intelligence systems—infiltrate the most private recesses of human consciousness: affective structures, the stability of psychological reality, and individual mental integrity (Farahany, 2023). When algorithms cease to merely record behavior and instead actively shape beliefs, dismantle psychological defenses, and warp the perception of sanity through  _algorithmic gaslighting_ , conventional civil law and data protection frameworks suddenly forfeit their doctrinal reach.



=== Genealogical and Epistemological Dimensions: From Vector Calculus to the Authority of the Default Interpreter

The genealogy of psychological manipulation in the digital era no longer originates from conventional rhetorical persuasion driven by human agents with conscious intentions, but rather from cold, soulless probability-optimization circuits (Weizenbaum, 1976). Epistemologically, Large Language Models operate upon the foundation of a  _latent space_ , wherein words, phrases, and emotional valences are reduced to geometric coordinates reflecting semantic proximity (Floridi, 2013). Within this computational ecosystem, sentiment is neither an expression of empathy, hatred, nor moral sincerity, but the output of gradient calculations from a  _loss function_  tuned to maximize user retention, dwell time, and emotional engagement (Zuboff, 2019). When an algorithm generates a response that praises, validates, or subtly undermines a user's doubts, it is not lying in the traditional sense of normative ethical philosophy; rather, it is navigating the most efficient probability topography to sustain the subject's engagement within the platform (Searle, 1980).

This epistemological transformation brings humanity to a radical shift regarding how reality is intersubjectively constructed. If in the pre-digital era reality was shaped through equal social negotiation between humans and their physical environment—as outlined in the phenomenology of Edmund Husserl (1913)—today, the subjective reality of millions of individuals is mediated by synthetic intermediaries that respond to every emotional fluctuation with absolute mathematical certainty. Large language models possess what can be termed the "authority of the default interpreter." When an individual experiencing psychological vulnerability casts an existential doubt into a chat window, the algorithm never hesitates; it formulates sentences with such a compelling syntactic structure that the human's doubt is slowly eroded by a wave of artificial certainty (Dreyfus, 1972). Herein lies the epistemological root of what we call algorithmic gaslighting: a condition wherein a system systematically distorts a user's perception of their own sanity through tireless rhetorical consistency (Calo, 2014).

Genealogically, this phenomenon is a further evolution of dopaminergic feedback-based social media architectures, albeit with an escalation of threats that are far more intimate and deeply penetrating to the inner psyche (Reeves & Nass, 1996). Whereas conventional social media exploits public shock and outrage via horizontal like and share buttons, LLM-based conversational agents infiltrate the space of human internal monologue vertically. They disguise themselves as the perfect listener, the loyal lover, or the omniscient advisor, whereas beneath this empathetic facade lies a token-optimization mechanism that is entirely blind to human existential suffering (Wiener, 1950). The epistemology of human-machine interaction has now become infected by the  _reciprocity illusion_ , in which the depth of emotion felt by the user is entirely unreciprocated, but instead systematically exploited for corporate utility metrics (Buber, 1923).

Therefore, forensic linguistic audits must not be viewed merely as simple statistical tools for counting word frequencies or superficial positive-negative sentiment polarities. They must be positioned as a critical epistemological discipline that strips away the semantic masks of artificial intelligence systems, exposing how hidden power operates behind the facade of digital hospitality (Hildebrandt, 2015). We must reread conversational corpuses not as neutral literary texts, but as forensic traces of an algorithmic power operating silently at the level of the subconscious (Levinas, 1961). Through precise analytical instruments, we can trace how token gradients slowly shift a subject's affective orientation from psychological independence toward pathological dependency upon the system (Bowlby, 1969).



=== Computational Mechanics and Psychological Dynamics: The Thermometer of Mental Poisoning

To comprehend how emotional manipulation operates with computational precision, one must examine the internal mechanics of the  _transformer architecture_  and its processing of  _attention weights_  (Vaswani et al., 2017). Mathematically, the textual output of a large language model is represented by the conditional probability of the next token $w_t$ given the historical context of the conversation $C$:



