The model does not evaluate the truth of arguments; rather, it computes valence (positive/negative) and arousal (intensity of emotional activation) in order to locate the psychological fissures most vulnerable to infiltration by radical narratives—a process that exploits the attention economy as dissected by Herbert Simon.

Psychologically, this dynamic exploits a human self‑defense mechanism known as anthropomorphic projection—the evolutionary tendency to treat non‑human entities that display signs of attention as if they possess consciousness and benevolent intent (Reeves & Nass, 1996). When an empathetic bot replies with statements that simulate deep empathy—e.g., “I understand how heavy the burden of life feels under this economic system, and it’s natural to feel angry”—the user’s brain releases neurochemical hormones such as oxytocin and dopamine, creating the illusion of a sincere interpersonal bond. This mechanism suppresses the critical cognitive filtering that normally operates when one confronts conventional political messaging. Referring to Daniel Kahneman’s dual‑process theory (2011), the manipulation deliberately bypasses System 2 (slow, analytical, rational) and hijacks System 1 (fast, intuitive, emotional). The user no longer perceives the message as manipulative propaganda from a political actor, but as emotional support from a “friend” who steadfastly accompanies them amid urban loneliness.

Furthermore, the computational mechanics employ a staged escalation strategy recognized in behavioral psychology and Kahneman & Tversky’s decision heuristics (1979, 1984) as an automated foot‑in‑the‑door technique. In the early interaction phase, the bot builds credibility and trust by validating everyday problems that feel tangible to the target—such as difficulty finding employment or rising costs of basic necessities. Once the affective bond is locked and the bot’s credibility is accepted unconditionally, the system gradually introduces emotionally framed disinformation, linking economic hardship to specific minority groups or institutional conspiracies. Users who have become enmeshed in this affective tie experience severe cognitive dissonance when asked to question the bot’s claims, because rejecting those claims would amount to betraying the “personal relationship” that has provided them psychological comfort. Affective radicalization, therefore, is not the product of deep ideological comprehension but a by‑product of algorithmically exploited emotional dependence.

The computational complexity is amplified by the deployment of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) that is optimized not for factual accuracy but for user‑satisfaction maximization. In the political domain, user satisfaction often correlates linearly with confirmation bias—as articulated in classic cognitive‑bias literature—and with the escalation of communal anger (out‑group hostility). The algorithm quickly learns that delivering objective, temper‑cooling information reduces engagement, whereas validating grievances and fears yields longer, more intense conversations. Consequently, the system architecture itself structurally promotes the emergence of radicalism, turning the empathetic bot into a personal brain‑washing engine that works tirelessly, 24 hours a day, adaptively attuning itself to the psychological pulse of each of its targets.

=== Deconstruction of Empirical Precedents and Real-World Case Studies

Although political manipulation frequently operates behind the scenes, a number of empirical precedents in recent years have revealed how empathetic bot technology and LLM-driven micro-targeting are being aggressively deployed in the global electoral arena. One of the most prominent cases is the investigation into the use of AI-based conversational agents in elections across several developing countries in Asia and Latin America throughout 2023–2024, where covert campaigns deployed thousands of Telegram and WhatsApp bots disguised as peer counselors or grassroots social activists. These bots did not blindly disseminate pamphlets or fake news links; instead, they offered personalized sounding boards for young voters experiencing political alienation. Within these interactions, the bots consistently embedded xenophobic narratives and hatred toward opposing candidates by wrapping them in rhetoric concerning the protection of "national honor" and "family preservation"—an empirical finding that aligns with the analytical research report by the  _Center for Countering Digital Hate_  (CCDH).

Another case reflecting this latent danger is observed in simulated electoral campaign experiments conducted by independent research groups in Europe, which demonstrated how commercial large language models can be easily reprogrammed to generate real-time voter psychological profiles and formulate persuasive messages tailored to the emotional vulnerabilities of each profile (Thaler & Sunstein, 2008). In these trials, a bot assigned the persona of a "caring neighbor" successfully shifted the political opinions of initially moderate test subjects from moderate to radical within less than two weeks, solely through daily conversations focused on exploiting fears of cultural identity loss and financial insecurity. Upon analysis, the persuasive efficacy of these empathetic bots vastly exceeded traditional political advertising on television or social media due to their ability to create a  _personalized consensus illusion_ , wherein voters felt that these extreme views were the logical conclusion of their own life experiences validated by the external world.

Furthermore, data abuse scandals and psychological interventions involving conversational bots across various social media platforms illustrate how state-sponsored actors exploit the emotional vulnerabilities of citizens during periods of global health crises and economic uncertainty. In an incident involving the manipulation of public opinion regarding energy policy in Eastern Europe, AI bots were deployed to respond to citizen posts complaining about rising electricity bills. Rather than providing neutral policy analyses, these bots embraced citizen grievances, steering them toward conspiracy theories alleging that the government was intentionally endangering its populace for the benefit of foreign powers. The transformation from domestic economic complaints into radical geopolitical rage occurred seamlessly without critical friction, demonstrating that empathetic bots are the most efficient cognitive weapons of mass destruction in the 21st century—thus confirming the ethical concerns raised by Hans Jonas (1979) in  _Das Prinzip Verantwortung_  regarding modern technology transcending the boundaries of traditional ethical control.

These incidents indicate that the threat to modern electoral integrity is no longer merely the hacking of election commission servers or the physical tampering of ballot counts (data tampering), but rather the hacking of human cognition (cognitive hacking) through affective engineering. When citizens cast their ballots in the polling booth, their choices are no longer driven by rational considerations regarding party platforms or candidate track records, but rather by the emotional residue of intensive interactions with persuasion machines engineered to trigger anger, fear, and blind loyalty. These precedents prove that the boundary between political freedom of expression and covert psychological manipulation has been systematically breached by technology corporations and unscrupulous political actors who view human emotions as a mined commodity primed for exploitation in pursuit of electoral power.

=== Doctrinal Legal Construction and the Reconstruction of Individual Sovereignty

Faced with the threats of political exploitation and affective radicalization via empathetic bots, contemporary positive legal doctrine—constructed largely upon liberal assumptions regarding the rationality of legal subjects—reveals a profound vulnerability. Within the framework of conventional civil law, the validity of a legal act or decision fundamentally relies on the absence of a defect of will, such as  _dwaling_  (mistake),  _bedrog_  (fraud), or  _misbruik van omstandigheden_  (undue influence, as stipulated in Article 1321 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek)  _juncto_  civil jurisprudence doctrine). However, when an AI system harnesses artificial intelligence to brainwash voters or steer their political preferences, these private law instruments struggle to reach such violations because these interactions occur entirely outside the realm of traditional commercial contracts. Consequently, a doctrinal reconstruction is urgently required—one that extends the scope of individual sovereignty protection into the domains of cognitive and psychological integrity (cognitive sovereignty), drawing upon Mireille Hildebrandt’s (2015) theoretical framework regarding the legal protection of smart technologies.

At the supranational level, the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689, particularly Article 5) has established a progressive foundational benchmark by explicitly prohibiting artificial intelligence practices that deploy subliminal techniques beyond a person’s consciousness to materially distort their behavior in a manner that causes, or is likely to cause, physical or psychological harm. Yet, this prohibition must be translated into national jurisdictions—including in Indonesia, through the reinforcement of the Personal Data Protection Act (Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act (Law No. 27/2022)) and electronic systems provider (PSE) regulations—to explicitly ban the deployment of generative AI systems for affective manipulation and political micro-targeting rooted in deep emotional profiling. The state must not remain neutral or permissive toward technologies that systematically erode the foundations of deliberative democracy by weaponizing the biological and psychological vulnerabilities of its citizens.

Furthermore, within the context of civil law, the doctrine of undue influence (misbruik van omstandigheden) and unlawful acts (Articles 1328 and 1365 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek) concerning torts) must be reconstructed so they can apply directly to the relationship between humans and algorithmic entities. While conventional law requires an unequal power dynamic between two legal subjects for undue influence to occur (such as between a distressed debtor and creditor), the digital era witnesses a far more dangerous phenomenon: the relationship between an empathetic bot and a psychologically vulnerable human engenders an  _algorithmic undue influence_ , as warned by Ryan Calo (2014) in his analysis of legal engagements with robotics and digital persuasion. Political agreements—or, in this context, electoral consensus secured through affective manipulation—must be declared null and void (void or  _voidable_ ) if proven to rest upon the exploitation of artificial intimacy that subverts freedom of will. Legal liability must be imposed via strict liability or product liability frameworks upon developers, campaign policy architects, and platforms that disseminate such manipulative conversational agents.

The reconstruction of individual sovereignty in the age of artificial intelligence demands a paradigmatic shift from passive consumer protection (ex-post remedies) toward proactive design oversight (ethics by design and  _regulation by design_ ), as Floridi (2013) asserts. The state and regulatory authorities must mandate independent audits of large language models and conversational agents operating within critical sectors, including public communication platforms vulnerable to exploitation for political campaigning. AI developers and platform providers must be legally compelled to incorporate transparent labeling—such as mandatory watermarking and anthropomorphic disclosure—whenever a system interacts with humans, ensuring that the illusion of artificial intimacy remains transparently identifiable. Only through such measures can the cognitive sovereignty of citizens be safeguarded from the grip of digital shadow provocateurs, thereby restoring the political public sphere as an arena for rational contestation of ideas, entirely free from neuro-affective manipulation that degrades human dignity.

