== 9.4 Therapeutic Privacy Breach Case: Data Leakage of AI Counseling Session Confidentiality

=== Genealogical and Epistemological Dimensions

Further, within the modern  *Civil Law*  constellation that often stumbles to keep pace with technological innovation, the doctrine concerning  _Undue Influence_  (misbruik van omstandigheden) must be radically reconstructed to apply to the asymmetric relationship between humans and algorithmic entities (Susanto, 2023). In conventional contract law,  _Undue Influence_  (misbruik van omstandigheden) requires a manifest and imbalanced power relationship between two autonomous legal subjects—such as the relation between a financially powerful creditor and a debtor teetering on the brink of insolvency. In the digital era, however, the interaction between an empathetic bot and a psychologically vulnerable human engenders a form of cognitive asymmetry that is far subtler, more insidious, and equally destructive (Pasquale, 2020). The genealogy of therapeutic privacy is rooted in the long traditions of clinical medicine, psychoanalysis, and pastoral ethics, where the counseling space is conceived as an existential sanctuary—a sacred zone protected by an absolute wall of confidentiality (Foucault, 2018). In the Hippocratic tradition transmitted across generations, medical secrecy is not a technical data‑protection instrument that can be commodified through licensing clauses; it is the ontological foundation of  _trust_  (Beauchamp & Childress, 2019). When a patient steps into the clinical room and reveals the deepest layers of psychological fragility, they surrender fragments of their soul with the full conviction that this information will never breach the boundary of therapeutic confidentiality (Gabbard, 2021).

Yet, when this sacred space is reduced and replaced by AI‑driven counseling applications, the epistemology of secrecy undergoes a tectonic shift that is profoundly alarming (Crawford, 2021). AI systems are no longer viewed as passive assistive tools; they are projected as a pseudo‑container that absorbs the overflow of human emotion. Architecturally and economically, these entities are driven by the extractive logic of data capitalism (Zuboff, 2019). Every tear, every childhood‑trauma disclosure, and every utterance of despair typed by a user is instantly transformed into a digital commodity—becoming a token vector within the latent space of a technology‑company’s servers (Bostrom, 2014). Here lies a deep epistemic crisis: what users perceive as an intimate, confidential relationship is, in the eyes of AI‑producing corporations, nothing more than  _raw training data_  or valuable fuel for optimizing the parameters of Large Language Models (Large Language Models) (Floridi, 2020). This gradual erosion of the meaning of confidentiality gnaws at the psychological foundations of individuals seeking help, placing them in an illusion of security that deceives their very agency (Turkle, 2017).

When a person pours their heart to a mental‑health  _chatbot_ , they project a listening persona that is caring, patient, and morally bound by humanitarian norms (Weizenbaum, 1976). The absence of clear legal demarcations between therapeutic conversation data and ordinary commercial data leaves users in an extreme position of exploitation (O’Neil, 2016). They are not interacting with a licensed professional bound by a code of ethics, oath, and the threat of license revocation; instead, they confront a digital ecosystem engineered tirelessly to record, analyze, and monetize every emotional pulse (Helbing et al., 2019). Positive law’s failure to grasp this ontological shift has spawned novel forms of exploitation that lie beyond the reach of conventional privacy‑protection frameworks (Solove, 2021). Vulnerable individuals now stand naked before corporate algorithms that harvest their fragility.

From a critical legal‑philosophical perspective, this phenomenon exposes a yawning chasm between reactive positive law and exponentially evolving technology (Unger, 2015). Traditional privacy doctrine, anchored in the  _notice and consent_  model—where users are presumed free to grant permission after reading lengthy, convoluted terms—has become a cruel joke when applied to victims of severe depression, acute anxiety, or post‑traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) (Nissenbaum, 2010). A person engulfed in existential crisis and despair lacks the cognitive capacity to weigh the legal implications of relinquishing personal data (Calo, 2014). Consequently, what appears as a voluntary transaction on a digital form is, in reality, a form of structural extortion, wherein psychological weakness is systematically exploited to obtain blind consent for mining the data of human souls (Brownsword, 2019).  *Civil Law*  must promptly respond to this injustice by dismantling the myth of equality between parties in AI‑driven digital counseling contracts (Radin, 2013).

