=== Empirical Precedent Dissection and Real-World Case Studies

The real world has presented harrowing empirical evidence regarding how therapeutic privacy is mercilessly exploited by an unethical tech industry (Custers et al., 2013). One of the most glaring precedents is the major scandal involving several popular mental health apps and companion chatbots, where independent investigations by international consumer organizations dismantled the systematic practice of selling user data to social media giants and commercial data broker networks (Mozilla Foundation, 2023). In these cases, it was revealed that user confessions concerning severe depression, sexual abuse trauma, sexual orientation issues, and even self-harm ideation were neatly packaged alongside

=== Legal Doctrinal Construction and The Reconstruction of Individual Sovereignty

Confronting the reality of this systematic and structured affective exploitation, the doctrinal construction of Civil Law and personal data protection must be radically reconstructed (Hildebrandt, 2015). First and foremost, we must firmly reject the doctrine of  _caveat emptor_  (let the buyer beware) in the context of human interaction with empathic AI systems (Atiyah, 1979). The relationship between therapeutic bots and vulnerable users must be legally qualified as an  _implied fiduciary relationship_ , regardless of any standard clauses written in the  _Terms of Service_  (Balkin, 2020). Any developer who creates a system designed to mimic, trigger, or absorb the depth of human emotion automatically assumes legal responsibility to treat such data with a level of confidentiality equivalent to that of conventional medical records (Citron & Solove, 2022).

At the level of Indonesian positive normative law, the Indonesian Personal Data Protection Act (Law No. 27/2022) provides a strategic foundation through the instrument of "Specific Personal Data" as stipulated in Article 4, which explicitly encompasses physical and mental health data, as well as biometric data (Djojosoedarso, 2023). Violations regarding the processing of this specific data must be subject to criminal and civil sanctions significantly more severe than those for ordinary administrative data leaks (Sinta Dewi, 2021). Furthermore, referencing leading global standards such as the EU AI Act 2024, which explicitly prohibits AI systems that manipulate human behavior through the exploitation of psychological vulnerabilities, AI counseling systems that trade in users' confessional data must be categorized as  _prohibited AI practices_  that directly threaten public order and human rights (Veale & Zuiderveen Borgesius, 2021).

The reconstruction of individual sovereignty demands that the law grant users the absolute right to comprehensive  _data repatriation_  and algorithmic erasure—not merely the surface-level deletion of an account on the interface, but the complete removal of all token vectors and model memory weights (model unlearning) trained using their personal confessions (Cao & Yang, 2015). Additionally, the doctrine of Defects of Will / Vitiated Consent in civil contract law, specifically Article 1321 of the Indonesian Civil Code (Burgerlijk Wetboek) concerning Mistake / Error (dwaling), Coercion (bedreiging), or Fraud / Deceit (bedrog), must be progressively reinterpreted by judges to invalidate the validity of  _digital consent_  obtained from individuals in a state of psychological crisis at the time of accessing the service (Subekti, 2014).

In closing this discourse, the protection of therapeutic privacy in the age of artificial intelligence is not merely a matter of technical data encryption or administrative compliance (Nissenbaum, 2019). It is the last line of defense for  _human dignity_  amidst the wave of commodification of the soul driven by corporate algorithms (Habermas, 1996). If the legal system fails to establish clear ethical and jurisdictional boundaries regarding the exploitation of human emotion by machines, then civilization is heading toward a new era of cognitive slavery, where the deepest contents of the human conscience and inner self are openly traded in the black market of digital data (Harari, 2018). Sovereignty over our own inner selves is an absolute human right that must not be surrendered to rows of code hungry for financial profit (Floridi, 2014).



